# Repository Engineering Instructions

When designing, implementing, modifying, or refactoring production code, apply the `engineering-standard` skill.

For any non-trivial feature, risky refactor, public API/contract change, persistence/schema change, cross-module change, authentication/authorization change, concurrency change, or retry/idempotency/transaction change, run the `feature-review` skill before considering the work complete.

Reviewers are read-only. The implementation agent validates each finding and remains responsible for fixes.

Prefer existing coherent repository conventions over introducing a second competing architecture.

Run the relevant formatter, linter/static analysis, tests, and build/type checks before claiming completion when available.


## Shared utility convention

Focused generic shared utilities such as `StringUtil`, `TimeUtil`, `DateUtil`, `CollectionUtil`, `JsonUtil`, and `EncodingUtil` are allowed and should be reused instead of reimplemented across modules.

Do not create vague `CommonUtil`, `CommonService`, or generic `Helper` dumping grounds. Keep business-specific helpers/rules in their owning module, and keep I/O/integration behavior in explicit services/clients/repositories/adapters.
